  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1143 AM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates an upper level shortwave trough lifting northeast\nthrough the Northern High Plains. Near the surface, a lee side trough\nof low pressure is shifting slowly east through extreme southeast\nColorado.\n\nAn ongoing dry pattern will persist early in the period as the REFS \nindicates an upper level shortwave trough exiting northeast through\nthe Northern Plains tonight, giving way to a southwesterly flow aloft\nacross the Western High Plains downstream of a deeper upper level\ntrough pushing off the Pacific into northern/central California.\nWith a lack of moisture return lending to little if any instability,\nprecip chances are expected to remain absent across the region through\nFriday night. Well above seasonal temperatures are likely again tonight\nas prevailing southerlies reinforce a milder air mass across the\nWestern High Plains. The latest REFS indicates a 70-90% probability\nof temperatures dropping below 40F in extreme southwest Kansas with\nonly a 30-50% probability of temperatures slipping below 45F across\nthe remainder of southwest Kansas into central Kansas where low level\nstratus is projected to develop overnight. Therefore, look for lows\nranging from the 30s(F) out west to the 40s(F) east. Similar high\ntemperatures from today are forecast again Thursday with little change\nto the general air mass across the high plains of western Kansas.\nExpect widespread afternoon highs in the 60s(F) with the REFS painting\na 70-90% probability of temperatures exceeding 60F. Similar highs\nare likely again Friday.",
